      "name": null,
      "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{9{x}^{3}-4x}{3{x}^{2}+2x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{9{x}^{3}-4x}{3{x}^{2}+2x} & =\\frac{9(0)^{3}-4(0)}{3(0)^{2}+2(0)}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9{x}^{3}-4x}{3{x}^{2}+2x} & =\\frac{x(9{x}^{2}-4)}{x(3x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9{x}^{2}-4}{3x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{9{x}^{3}-4x}{3{x}^{2}+2x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{9{x}^{2}-4}{3x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-4}{0+2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
