      "name": null,
      "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-3}{{x}^{2}-1}.$",
      "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-3}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}-2(-1)-3}{(-1)^{2}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-3}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-3)}{(x-1)(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-3}{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-3}{{x}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{x-3}{x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-3}{-1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{-2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
